  politics and diplomacy and whatever  else so what could the impact be of all  of this on the Democratic Party well the  Democratic Party as we know it could die  and by that I mean the DNC is a business  it also ran the primary like a business  they chose the product ahead of time as  opposed to letting the buyers decide  fairly they lied to people about their  role in choosing the product saying hey  we're not here to pick candidates we're  just here to run the primary we later  found out they were actually putting  their hands on the scale for Hillary  Clinton and the Democratic Party Pat has  been hugely discredited in a number of  different ways including that dishonest  primary but the real issue now is can  the Democratic Party reframe in a way  that at least relates more to the  support we saw for Bernie Sanders and  there was a deep desire for a candidate  like Bernie Sanders and less like  Hillary Clinton doesn't mean a hundred  percent Bernie maybe it means 60 Bernie  forty Hillary or something like that and  that's going to be a big thing for the  next two and four years my own things  have changed in the past couple of weeks  I mean we thought that the other  Republicans would have a really tough  time winning a national election again  and that Paul Ryan was gonna lose the  speakership and now things have just  shifted completely we didn't even get  the Senate it's a very very different  scenario and there's also questions  about the future of the Republican Party  right I mean the Republican Party is  clearly not dead because as Pat mentions  they won the house they won the Senate  and the White House however what is the  impact going to be of a Donald  presidency on the party you had some  major Republicans not supporting Trump  some of them have come back around  partially because Donald Trump seems to  be at least intimating to them that they  might be in the running for some nice  cushy positions Republican voters voted  for the guy who is a Republican by name  but also supports some major policies  that Republicans typically don't want I  mentioned major infrastructure spending  I have talked in the past about not  getting involved in the Middle East to  the same degree that neoconservatives  have liked to over the last couple of  decades and then the final question is  what happens to liberal media I'm not  going to get into that again because I  gave my sort of liberal media rant  earlier in the show I will remind you  that our program has really exploded  since the election of Donald Trump on  November 8th on every platform setting  records every single week records for  paid subscribers some of the other  progressive independent house have  reported similar upticks  up ticks and we have to see whether this  remains during the donald trump  presidency or whether this is just sort  of an initial push an initial excitement  from progressives because they realize a  lot is at stake here that may wane I  hope that that doesn't happen  we're going to be here certainly and  this is going to be not only to try to  push the President to not make  disastrous decisions but also to push  the Democratic Party to make good  decisions for the next couple of  election cycles
 

